Varicose Veins
Cardiovascular
Dilated tortuous superficial veins, Aching/heaviness exacerbated by standing, Relief with leg elevation, Superficial venous reflux on ultrasound

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CDV-045
Reason for Visit: Severe, throbbing pain and heaviness in the lower legs, with a new area of hard, tender redness over a bulging vein.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported chronic bulging leg veins now complicated by acute, localized pain, redness, and a hard lump. This presentation raises high suspicion for superficial thrombophlebitis. Urgent in-person physical assessment was required to accurately diagnose the condition, clinically rule out a concurrent deep vein thrombosis (DVT) or spreading cellulitis, and initiate appropriate anti-inflammatory therapy before the next available routine appointment.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My leg veins always ache by the end of the day, but today one of the bulging veins has turned into a hard, red, agonizingly painful cord."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a long-standing history of dilated, tortuous, and elongated superficial veins visible on both lower extremities. Experiences chronic dull aching, heaviness, and cramping in the affected legs, with symptoms worsening significantly after prolonged periods of standing at work. Relief of discomfort is usually achieved upon elevating the legs. Over the last 24 hours, the patient developed severe, localized tenderness and pain along the course of a varicosity on the left medial calf. The area is red and intensely pruritic. KEY SYMPTOMS: Dilated, tortuous superficial veins. Dull aching, heaviness in the affected legs. Symptoms worsening after prolonged periods of standing. Relief of discomfort upon elevating the legs. Localized tenderness or pain along the course of the varicosities.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently limping to avoid putting pressure on the left leg.
Social/Safety Concerns: Occupational requirement for prolonged standing is worsening the disease progression.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 | HR: 78 | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100 percent | Wt: 85 Kg
General: Uncomfortable, shifting weight off the left leg, afebrile.
Physical Exam: Extremities: Multiple large, dilated, and tortuous varicosities visible on the bilateral lower extremities (great saphenous distribution). On the left medial calf, there is a distinct 5cm segment of a superficial vein that is palpably firm (cord-like), warm, erythematous, and exquisitely tender to touch, consistent with superficial thrombophlebitis. Crucially, there is no massive unilateral calf swelling, no generalized calf muscle tenderness (negative Homan's sign), and no pitting edema extending to the knee to suggest a DVT. Mild venous eczema (dry, scaling skin) and early hemosiderin staining noted near the ankles. DIAGNOSTIC TETRAD: Dilated tortuous superficial veins, Aching/heaviness exacerbated by standing, Relief with leg elevation, Superficial venous reflux on ultrasound (anticipated on formal vascular imaging).
Environmental Observations: Patient wearing tight, unsupportive socks with elastic bands that constrict venous return.
Medication Reconciliation: Using over-the-counter paracetamol with minimal relief for the acute inflammation.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Varicose Veins complicated by Acute Superficial Thrombophlebitis - Status: Exacerbated (Inflamed)
Differentials:

Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The inflammation is strictly localized to a visible, superficial varicosity without the massive, generalized, unilateral calf swelling or deep muscle ache that characterizes a DVT.

Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The patient is afebrile, and the erythema is strictly linear following the course of a specific vein, rather than a broad, spreading plaque of hot, red skin.

Peripheral Arterial Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Strong, easily palpable pedal pulses, and symptoms that are relieved by elevation (venous) rather than worsened by elevation (arterial).
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with classic chronic venous insufficiency (varicose veins, aching relieved by elevation) now complicated by a classic acute superficial thrombophlebitis (a tender, red, palpable superficial cord). The absence of generalized calf swelling and deep tenderness makes a concurrent DVT highly unlikely clinically. Urgent after-hours assessment was necessary to explicitly differentiate this superficial, benign inflammation from a limb- or life-threatening DVT, and to prescribe robust anti-inflammatory medications to manage the severe, acute pain, safely averting an emergency department visit.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed a short course of oral Non-Steroidal Anti-Inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs), such as Ibuprofen 400mg TDS with food, to aggressively target the superficial venous inflammation. Advised topical application of a warm compress to the affected area.
Positive Behavioral Change: Instructed the patient to implement strict leg elevation (above the level of the heart) for 20 minutes every evening, and to immediately cease wearing socks with tight elastic bands. By utilizing gravity to drain the static blood, the patient directly reduces the venous pressure causing the ache. The immediate reward is profound relief of the heavy, throbbing sensation in the legs.
Home-Based Services: None.
DME/Safety: Strongly recommended the daily use of properly fitted, Class 2 graduated compression stockings (to be fitted once the acute inflammation subsides).
Follow-up: Routine General Practice review within 1 week to ensure the thrombophlebitis is resolving and to arrange a formal lower limb venous duplex ultrasound.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the entire calf suddenly becomes massively swollen, if the redness spreads rapidly across the whole leg, or if sudden chest pain or shortness of breath develops (indicating a clot traveling to the lungs), the patient must call an ambulance immediately.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; a formal ultrasound is required to definitively map the extent of venous reflux and completely rule out deep clots; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Superficial thrombophlebitis is painful but generally harmless and resolves over a few weeks with anti-inflammatories. Definitive treatment of the underlying varicose veins may require minor surgery.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Take the anti-inflammatories with food. Elevate your legs. Seek emergency care immediately if the whole leg swells massively or you develop chest pain.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: One of your bulging surface veins has developed a small, localized clot and severe inflammation. This is painful but it is NOT the dangerous type of deep blood clot. The anti-inflammatory medication will calm the redness and pain, and elevating your leg will help drain the trapped blood.
Actionable Steps: Start the Ibuprofen now. Prop your legs up on a stack of pillows. Do not wear tight socks that cut off your circulation.

